in Two-Dimensional Vector Fields" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050793 #edb/ref 1033 3716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050793 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "6768fffe-a644-5bd7-b35c-6b2cc2563786" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194303 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186194304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194303 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186194305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194303 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186194306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194303 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186194307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194303 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186194308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194303 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194303 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194303 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186194307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194303 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7d966562-9c87-57b1-a8bf-6b3edf2422f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194304 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194304 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194304 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b20b10fc-bb64-57ca-9ec9-e4d2df501a0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194305 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194305 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194305 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c439e078-219a-5d7c-931d-f4512f82a95f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194306 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194306 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194306 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c5449f6b-066e-5959-8004-2b1d283f4af0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194307 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194307 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194307 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f048743c-1904-5a19-bb5a-ec041bc93630" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194308 #edb/ref 1080 "-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194308 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194308 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "36e11e32-f25b-529c-abe5-51889402664c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198045 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12440" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198045 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f9/f9b1b286210d694cd3786fe38190216da503bfa3223114f86c8706f810313e33.png)\n\nThe curve $C_1$ has endpoints $A(5,3)$ and $B(1,3).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\sqrt{y^3}\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\sqrt{(5-x)^3}\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n through $C_1$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector  $\\mathbf{n}_1,$ as shown above. \n\n*Hint: You're given that $\\mathbf F$ is source-free.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198045 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7a42fb45-176b-5550-a206-41a92859987b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198045 #edb/ref 1074 "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $\\mathbf{F}$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0.$\n\n- The flux $\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s}$ along any closed curve $C \\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves $C_1, C_2 \\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}  \\,\\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}  \\,\\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $\\psi(x,y)$ for $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\sqrt{y^3}\\right) + \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(\\sqrt{(5-x)^3}\\right) = 0+0 = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $\\mathbf{F}$ is a source-free vector field.\n\nSince $\\mathbf{F}$ is source-free, we know the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ is independent of the path. In particular,\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\,\\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\,\\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $C_2$ is the straight line from $A(5,3)$ to $B(1,3)$ and $\\mathbf{n}_2$ is its unit normal, shown in the diagram below. \n\n![](images/2a/2aeb26e58251923821d4058f886eada6a29b473ea70714b32e98c5338ddd0b28.png)\n\nNotice that both $\\mathbf n_1$ and $\\mathbf n_2$ are $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotations of their respective unit tangent vectors.\n\nTo calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C_2,$ we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\,\\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_{C_2} P\\,\\textrm{d}y - Q\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply the formula,\n\n- the curve should be parametrized as $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t \\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_2$ *must* be a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of the unit normal tangent vector $\\mathbf{T}(t).$\n\nParametrizing $C_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =5\\,i+3\\,j+t\\cdot [(i+3\\,j)-(5\\,i+3\\,j)] \\\\  & =(5-4t)\\,i+3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,1].$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}=\\sqrt{(3)^{3}}=3\\sqrt{3}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =\\sqrt{(5-x)^{3}}=\\sqrt{(5-(5-4t))^{3}}=8\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5-4t)=-4, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the flux integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3\\sqrt{3})\\cdot (0)-(8\\sqrt{{t}^{3}})\\cdot (-4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}32{t}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{64{t}^{5/2}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{64(1)^{5/2}}{5})-(\\frac{(64(0)^{5/2}}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1\\, \\textrm{d}s = \\dfrac{64}{5}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198242 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9575" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198242 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = 4y(x^2+y^2)\\,\\mathbf{i} - 4x(x^2+y^2)\\,\\mathbf{j},$ which of the following statements must be true?\n\nI. $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F= 0$\n\nII. $\\mathbf{F}$ is a source-free vector field\n\nIII. For two arbitrary curves $C_1$ and $C_2,$ we have $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s \\neq \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198242 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "366c826f-6f15-5a21-9eaa-d74b6e4d8833" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198242 #edb/ref 1074 "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $\\mathbf{F}$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0.$\n\n- The flux $\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s}$ along any closed curve $C \\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves $C_1,C_2 \\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}  \\,\\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}  \\,\\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $\\psi(x,y)$ for $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nNote that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ is a vector field with the simply connected domain $D=\\mathbb{R}^2,$ where \n\n$$\nP(x,y) = 4y(x^2+y^2),\\qquad Q(x,y) = - 4x(x^2+y^2).\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=8xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-8xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\n=\n8xy + (-8xy) = 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. This follows from statement I.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\mathbf F$ is source-free, we have that $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s$ for all curves *with the same start and endpoints*.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198368 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198368 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198368 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198368 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198368 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198368 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198368 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198368 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198368 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198368 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198368 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198368 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198368 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying True Statements Regarding Source-Free Vector Fields" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198368 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198368 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c2dcf4be-ea5a-5ab3-8919-e61da330fa85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198403 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198403 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198403 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198403 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198403 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198403 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198403 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372188 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198403 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198403 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198403 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating Flux in a Source-Free Vector Field" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198403 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198403 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "fd9aad0a-f415-577c-9cb0-15044e4a43fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198492 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198492 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198492 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198492 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198492 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198492 #edb/ref 1045 "Source-Free Vector Fields" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198492 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186199318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198492 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198492 #edb/ref 1050 600.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198492 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "741f534f-c497-52b3-a525-05d9bf7f9e8f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198612 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198612 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161439" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198612 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198612 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/07/07aaf788552b002f804b98558a79a2951ac091897539093bbffc0063a2289897.png)\n\nThe curve ${C}_{1}$ has endpoints $A(1,-1)$ and $B(-1,1).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i+(1-y)\\,j$ through ${C}_{1}$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector ${n}_{1},$ as shown above.\n\n*Hint: You're given that $F$ is source-free, and the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B$ can be parametrized as*\n\n$$\nr=(1-2t)\\,i+(-1+2t)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198612 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bdff88fa-fe4d-5059-a512-6bd7bc96d899" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198612 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C\\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}\\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-y)=1-1=0,\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\nSince $F$ is source-free, we know the flux of $F$ is independent of the path. In particular, as we have that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{2}$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B,$ and ${n}_{2}$ is the unit normal to ${C}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/e2/e22757cf7c4e2be17cb71e63c550aa39a10ee1dc5895c63acd5a45f0baa94623.png)\n\nNotice that both ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotations of their respective unit tangent vectors.\n\nTo calculate the flux of $F$ across ${C}_{2},$ we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply the formula,\n\n- the curve should be parametrized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector ${n}_{2}$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit normal tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nWe have the parametrization\n\n$$\nr(t)=(1-2t)\\,i+(-1+2t)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=1-2t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =1-y=1-(2t-1)=2-2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t)=-2, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the flux integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-2t)\\cdot (2)-(2-2t)\\cdot (-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-2t)+(2-2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}3-4t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\cdot {[3t-2{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (3-2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199265 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199265 #edb/ref 1061 35868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199265 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2c5a36f7-0cf5-56e0-9ad2-156e162e6abd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199265 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199318 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199318 #edb/ref 1061 44699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199318 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3e2deeb1-79f4-5a70-897d-e88547dd9d33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199318 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199319 #edb/ref 1061 44701 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199319 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c4b926bf-0635-5002-9a4a-976c76662c96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199319 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199328 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199328 #edb/ref 1061 44869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199328 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b606814b-0712-51ca-ad68-9fac2299c246" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199328 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199478 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)= P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nwhich is defined on an open set $D\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2.$ Recall that a function $\\psi(x,y)$ is said to be a **stream function** of $\\mathbf F$ if it satisfies\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y}, \\qquad \nQ = -\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nIf a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ has a stream function $\\psi(x,y),$ then $\\mathbf{F}$ is called a **source-free vector field**. \n\nSource-free vector fields have some interesting properties, summarized below:\n\n- *The divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ at any point of $D$ is zero:* \n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} = \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0\n$$\n\n- *The *flux* of $\\mathbf{F}$ along any closed curve $C \\in D$ is zero:*\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s = 0\n$$\n\n- *Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves $C_1, C_2 \\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that*\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}  \\,\\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}  \\,\\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nStream functions for source-free vector fields play a similar role to potential functions for conservative vector fields. So it's worth comparing them side-by-side.\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" colspan=\"2\">Source-Free</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"left\" width=\"450\">Stream Function $\\psi$\n</td><td align=\"center\">$\\mathbf F =  \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathbf i -  \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathbf j$\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"left\">Relationship between $\\psi$ and $\\mathbf F$\n</td><td align=\"center\">$\\mathbf F$ is tangential to $\\psi$'s level curves\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"left\">Divergence is zero\n</td><td align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0$\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"left\">Flux is zero along closed curves\n</td><td align=\"center\">$\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s = 0$\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"left\">Flux integrals are path independent\n</td><td align=\"center\">$\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s$\n</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n<td width=\"30\"></td>\n<td>\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" colspan=\"2\">Conservative</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"left\" width=\"450\">Potential Function $\\phi$\n</td><td align=\"center\">$\\mathbf F =  \\dfrac{\\partial \\phi}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathbf i +  \\dfrac{\\partial \\phi}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathbf j$\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"left\">Relationship between $\\phi$ and $\\mathbf F$\n</td><td align=\"center\">$\\mathbf F$ is perpendicular to $\\phi$'s level curves\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"left\">(Scalar) curl is zero\n</td><td align=\"center\"> $\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 0$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"left\">Circulation is zero along closed curves\n</td><td align=\"center\">$\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf{r}= 0$\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"left\">Line integrals are path independent\n</td><td align=\"center\">$\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf{r} = \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf{r}$\n</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199478 #edb/ref 1065 7703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199478 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "69005432-5588-5ac3-bf47-9dd734bb6953" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199478 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199485 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's use what we know about source-free vector fields to calculate the flux of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=2y\\,\\mathbf{i}-2x\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nacross the curve $C,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/d6/d6adda2809af5d49c6b3dd0e81eae913dcdf02ceec0ae919fda0011d8a3dad2b.png)\n\nFirst, notice that this is a source-free vector field, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2x) \\\\  & =0+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\mathbf{F}$ is source-free, the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across any closed curve is zero. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s\n=\n0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199485 #edb/ref 1065 7751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199485 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "447855fc-5a5c-5450-99a6-91de0ed2203b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199485 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429347 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429347 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate f