] [17592186435332 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186435317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435332 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1f879c13-dbbc-55d7-b911-a1fc2b3863d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435333 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435333 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435333 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435333 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435333 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435333 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435333 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435333 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186435327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435333 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "984d6c5b-f37c-575f-adde-0cd67c2d7720" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435334 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435334 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162590" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435334 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435334 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/5f/5fa49c55e5741ccf46a2c77dd5e1770a8e5e7b89a29641af44e42eecbbb82f17.png)\n\nThe curve ${C}_{1}$ has endpoints $A(2,-1)$ and $B(-1,1).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=(2x+y)\\,i+(-2y)\\,j$ through ${C}_{1}$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector ${n}_{1},$ as shown above.\n\n*Hint: You're given that $F$ is source-free, and the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B$ can be parametrized as*\n\n$$\nr=(-3t+2)\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435334 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "21126858-0865-5434-a374-dbf17e23e496" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435334 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C\\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}\\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y)=2-2=0,\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\nSince $F$ is source-free, we know the flux of $F$ is independent of the path. In particular, as we have that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{2}$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B,$ and ${n}_{2}$ is the unit normal to ${C}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/6b/6bb6e38f83006fa9874ca0020e4eb016ea8eed7fec79596e984b94a0f3853d75.png)\n\nNotice that both ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotations of their respective unit tangent vectors.\n\nTo calculate the flux of $F$ across ${C}_{2},$ we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply the formula,\n\n- the curve should be parametrized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector ${n}_{2}$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit normal tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nWe have the parametrization\n\n$$\nr(t)=(-3t+2)\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =2x+y=2(-3t+2)+(2t-1)=-4t+3, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-2y=-2(2t-1)=-4t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3t+2)=-3, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the flux integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-4t+3)\\cdot (2)-(-4t+2)\\cdot (-3)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-8t+6)+(6-12t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(12-20t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[12t-10{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(12-10) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435335 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435335 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162558" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435335 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435335 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i+2x{y}^{2}\\,j,$ which of the following statements must be true?\n\nI. $∇\\cdot F=0$\n\nII. There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F$\n\nIII. There exists a close curve $C$ such that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s\\ne 0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435335 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "568651db-e544-5f07-84be-3a27fc735107" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435335 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C⊂D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}⊂D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i+2x{y}^{2}\\,j$ is a vector field with the simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)={x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=2x{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2x+4xy=2x(1+2y)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Indeed, they follow from the fact that $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435336 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435336 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162554" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435336 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435336 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/62/62946451fd0305439366bdc22db84f8a5823481082e8ac39858369fbc582676d.png)\n\nWhat is the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j$ across the curve $C$ shown above?\n\n*Hint: Start by checking whether $F$ is source-free.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435336 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dd61f71d-b16d-557d-a304-ee717f93d77f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435336 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C⊂D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}⊂D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\  & =(1+x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $F$ is *not* a source-free vector field. \n\nSince $F$ is not source-free, we do not have enough information to determine the flux of $F$ through $C.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
